2186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fc79ba8-2e9f-5cc7-8c64-5ba490045273" :question/math-academy-id "q-181690" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/assets/883d270ae063f134ee0ac04f717bcc9b5800adb939be3c7f19f25602ad52360e.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-2\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-2<y\\le 2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<y\\le 2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e4aa80-0b18-5d61-92cc-09a3625afcae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368817} {:answer/id #uuid "deeb79a1-4d8e-509c-915f-894121624834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368818} {:answer/id #uuid "78a36867-6f64-5527-8d74-0837869c5c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368819} {:answer/id #uuid "d0336e32-9c9e-5412-9296-812b79187e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e4aa80-0b18-5d61-92cc-09a3625afcae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368817} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6313efc-774e-5136-b7f3-ce5f837cfe2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "998b3818-315b-5b25-8281-21d4bb2b7ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368822} {:answer/id #uuid "c529f59c-5905-5126-b2cc-23fbc1f667af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368823} {:answer/id #uuid "50758b39-91c1-57cf-8713-e80a03f8a180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368824} {:answer/id #uuid "198dd157-092d-56af-9733-19632bf4e178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368825} {:answer/id #uuid "1423901f-3ead-5043-936f-ebed308598a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "198dd157-092d-56af-9733-19632bf4e178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368825} :answer-field/id #uuid "d223cb66-4872-5918-b5e0-b58a4c692240" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3633038f-6f7f-5196-bcd7-797a232f08ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-181692" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/assets/7021cfd590d7e090128061d42c140ab81633f20e8cd1bff60f6252bc8e9aa882.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -{y}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, One Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed47bf61-9f12-53a7-a16c-0adc14b8e7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368829} {:answer/id #uuid "d43cf466-d4ae-5a98-aec1-024211220d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368830} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea9ffe8-9d4e-5120-9d4d-64da8ef5c971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368831} {:answer/id #uuid "c04c6aea-608c-5197-8405-dbdcb69d96ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368832} {:answer/id #uuid "d2aff669-07b9-559a-abcc-29f31fff5c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368833} {:answer/id #uuid "88436d1d-883a-5550-89d9-3f16007d216e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04c6aea-608c-5197-8405-dbdcb69d96ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368832} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94d7c5b-b09b-5389-99fe-dbed468ca8d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368828} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b51c7f-93a5-576d-9996-eab9b9e041d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368836} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe00bfd-18cd-53ba-ba2c-a4652130463b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368837} {:answer/id #uuid "ac47281d-54fd-57e3-bd12-0c0e9dfcfca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368838} {:answer/id #uuid "42d0eb41-0b89-5fdb-ba15-5e3769b316aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368839} {:answer/id #uuid "44707093-92f7-52ce-861e-83c3e9565532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44707093-92f7-52ce-861e-83c3e9565532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368840} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a378291-36f3-5534-9841-000847b38e3e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71c1be23-98af-5443-8ca0-30e1ee2b6297" :question/math-academy-id "q-182032" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/assets/980ae73286ed41ed0c6f074a4bed5b35fa852dd608fb0a1a561f26a7eac60ea7.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *to the left* the line $x=2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx<2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\nx<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,x<2}\n$$"}]}
